WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.051
The baseline: the comparison a reviewer will accept.

2
00:00:03.331 --> 00:00:06.083
A number without a comparison can’t be defended.

3
00:00:06.363 --> 00:00:11.376
“81 percent task success” invites the obvious question: compared with what?

4
00:00:11.656 --> 00:00:16.520
The baseline is your answer, and choosing it is a judgment call that a reviewer will check.

5
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.579
A good baseline is the simplest credible alternative the user could have instead.

6
00:00:21.859 --> 00:00:23.843
Usually that is one of three things.

7
00:00:24.123 --> 00:00:30.672
The current manual process, measured on a sample (how long it takes and how many errors a careful person makes).

8
00:00:30.952 --> 00:00:34.771
A non-AI tool: keyword search, a linter, a spreadsheet filter.

9
00:00:35.051 --> 00:00:43.797
Or a single model call with no retrieval, no tools, and no verification, which tells you what your architecture adds on top of the raw model.

10
00:00:44.077 --> 00:00:50.392
Pick the one that a skeptical reviewer would consider the real competition, and if two are plausible, run both.

11
00:00:50.672 --> 00:00:59.205
Run the baseline on the same eval cases with the same graders as your system, so you can report paired differences, the method from Studio 3.

12
00:00:59.485 --> 00:01:01.277
The baseline also protects you.

13
00:01:01.557 --> 00:01:11.669
If it already meets your success target, you have learned that in week 1 rather than week 6, and you can move to a harder slice of the problem where your system has room to prove itself.
